WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.400
-John, welcome back.
-Hey, Jimmy.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.234
Great to be back. Thank you
for having me, everybody.

00:00:05.234 --> 00:00:07.667
-Yeah, John Oliver, everybody.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:10.000
-Pleasure to be here.
-John...

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.000
-Ah, sorry.
How you been?

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.234
-I've --
[ Laughter ]

00:00:13.234 --> 00:00:14.868
What's new?
What's new? Anything?

00:00:14.868 --> 00:00:16.467
-You know,
it's nice to be back.

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:18.133
Work's -- Family's good.
-Yeah.

00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:19.601
Good. I'm so happy.
-Yeah.

00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:21.801
-What are you wearing?

00:00:21.801 --> 00:00:23.467
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, that's right.

00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:25.901
Um, I am, uh --

00:00:25.901 --> 00:00:29.534
I was told explicitly
this evening was bird-themed.

00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:31.234
-No, no, that's not true.
-No, that's not true.

00:00:31.234 --> 00:00:33.100
-Sorry about that, no.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.934
No, we did --
On our show on Sunday,

00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:38.968
we entered the Australian --

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:40.801
Sorry. Not Australian.
New Zealand.

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:43.100
I could not have made a more
offensive mistake than that.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.334
[ Laughter ]

00:00:44.334 --> 00:00:46.701
Although, "po-tay-toe,"
"po-tah-toe," am I right?

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:48.901
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:50.968
New Zealand's
Bird of the Year contest...

00:00:50.968 --> 00:00:52.701
-Ooh.
-...which is, this year is,

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:55.467
Bird of the Century.
It's a fantastic contest.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:57.300
Everyone votes on their
favorite bird,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.601
and their bird then becomes

00:00:58.601 --> 00:01:01.200
everyone's official
favorite bird.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.901
And we loved it.
Really charming contest.

00:01:04.901 --> 00:01:06.634
I looked to see if there
was one like it in the U.S.

00:01:06.634 --> 00:01:08.067
There just isn't.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.033
And then, the more we
looked at it, we realized it's

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:13.400
not actually restricted just
to New Zealand, this contest.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.400
Anyone can campaign for a bird.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.200
That seemed like a vulnerability
in their system

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.367
that somebody could exploit.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:22.701
We were happy
to be that somebody,

00:01:22.701 --> 00:01:24.701
and so we are now
the official campaign manager

00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:26.634
for the puteketeke,
which is what I am right now.

00:01:26.634 --> 00:01:29.901
[ Cheers and applause ]
It'll be great.

00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:33.634
-[ Laughs ]
-That's right.

00:01:33.634 --> 00:01:35.834
[ Cheering increases ]

00:01:35.834 --> 00:01:38.901
-What is?
-Well, it seems like --

00:01:38.901 --> 00:01:41.133
seems like quite a lot
of puteketeke fans out there.

00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.534
-Yes.
P-Puteketeke bird.

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:44.334
-Puteketeke.
I mean, even the --

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:46.801
even the name is just a dance
in your mouth, isn't it?

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:49.167
-Yeah. Puteketeke.
-Let your tongue

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:51.067
explore your face.
Puteketeke

00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:54.834
-And why -- why this bird?
Why the puteketeke?

00:01:54.834 --> 00:01:56.567
-Good question.

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:59.667
It's that we thought that
it was a magnificent bird.

00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:02.467
I believe,
as we described it on Sunday,

00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:04.300
it looks like
Pauly D [bleep] a swan.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.400
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.033
I don't think it's unfair to say

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:10.467
that's what you're
looking at right now.

00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:14.501
And so, it's a charming bird.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:17.667
Right. You could defend that
insult in court, could you not?

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:19.734
-Yes, yes, yes.
-And so --

00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:22.300
Yes.
So, it's an interesting bird.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.934
There's only a thousand of them
in New Zealand.

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:27.534
So I think it's a fine candidate
for Bird of the Century.

00:02:27.534 --> 00:02:29.167
-Did you contact
this organization

00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:31.868
running this contest
before you started?

00:02:31.868 --> 00:02:33.567
-We did, we did.
Yeah, we didn't just spring --

00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:35.033
We said to them,
"Would it be okay

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:38.067
if we were campaign managers
for a bird?"

00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:39.767
They said, "Yes, go for it."

00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:41.300
And I don't think
they understood

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.267
quite what they were unleashing
when they said, "Go for it."

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:44.467
[ Laughter ]
-Because you actually --

00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:46.367
You put up ads
now all over New Zealand.

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:48.734
-Oh, yeah, we've gone
absolutely nuclear on them.

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:50.934
So, now, what I did not --
Before you show it,

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:52.567
what I did not realize
going into this

00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:54.901
was that the the bird
that won last year won

00:02:54.901 --> 00:02:58.701
with just over 2,800 votes.
-2,800 votes.

00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:00.501
-Yeah.
-Mm.

00:03:00.501 --> 00:03:04.267
-Uh, I think we might beat that,
due to the things that we...

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:05.601
-[ Laughing ] Oh, my God.
-So, yeah,

00:03:05.601 --> 00:03:07.334
we put up billboards in --

00:03:07.334 --> 00:03:08.968
Well, first, we put up
a billboard

00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:11.801
in New Zealand,
places in Wellington.

00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:13.734
Lord of the Wings. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:13.734 --> 00:03:15.934
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.934 --> 00:03:19.234
-So that was --
that was just the start.

00:03:19.234 --> 00:03:20.601
-Lord of the Wings
is pretty good.

00:03:20.601 --> 00:03:21.968
-Lord of the Wings is --
Yeah, it's not bad.

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:23.601
It's a good starting point.
-Yeah. So...

00:03:23.601 --> 00:03:25.968
-From square one,
we then really went global.

00:03:25.968 --> 00:03:29.334
We put up billboards in --
Okay. This is Mumbai,

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:33.367
this is Tokyo, this is
the Champs-Elysées in Paris,

00:03:33.367 --> 00:03:36.767
this is London,
this is Ipanema Beach in Brazil,

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:38.834
and this, of course,
is Manitowoc, Wisconsin.

00:03:38.834 --> 00:03:40.634
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.634 --> 00:03:43.434
-You ended up in a little town
in Wisconsin, as well?

00:03:43.434 --> 00:03:45.033
-Yeah. Not everyone
lives in big cities.

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:48.367
-That's true. You're right.
That is -- That is unbelievable.

00:03:48.367 --> 00:03:50.567
This is unbelievable.
I love that you're doing this.

00:03:50.567 --> 00:03:52.300
You're like, "Oh, we're
gonna make this a thing."

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.133
And this bird is -- I think
this bird is gonna win.

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:55.667
Everyone who has
an e-mail address --

00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:57.701
-If you -- If --
Anyone can vote.

00:03:57.701 --> 00:04:01.901
You should vote.
If you go to votethisbird.com,

00:04:01.901 --> 00:04:05.601
pick the puteketeke
as your choice --

00:04:05.601 --> 00:04:07.834
as your first choice.
It will ask you to --

00:04:07.834 --> 00:04:09.334
You just need a valid e-mail.

00:04:09.334 --> 00:04:12.834
If you then validate that
e-mail, you will be part of

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:14.901
what I think is going
to be a landslide victory.

00:04:14.901 --> 00:04:18.000
It is American money
undermining a foreign election.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.133
What is more to be proud
about that?

00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:21.801
[ Cheers and applause ]
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:21.801 --> 00:04:25.567
-What?
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:25.567 --> 00:04:27.501
That is unbelievable.
You know, there --

00:04:27.501 --> 00:04:29.968
there are over --
[ Both laugh ]

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:33.400
-Now I keep -- I'm now realizing
why you keep lunging backwards.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:34.734
And it's 'cause
I've got a beak.

00:04:34.734 --> 00:04:36.000
-You have a beak, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.801
-Yeah, I hadn't --
-You came in, we were gonna hug,

00:04:37.801 --> 00:04:40.567
and you're not used
to having a beak, you know?

00:04:40.567 --> 00:04:41.801
-I nearly stabbed you
with a beak.

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:43.968
-Yes.
-What a way to go that would be.

00:04:43.968 --> 00:04:45.968
-This is the funniest --
I never thought I'd see it.

00:04:45.968 --> 00:04:49.801
Like, this is great.
But there's over 70 birds

00:04:49.801 --> 00:04:51.300
competing for
Bird of the Century.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.801
I just want to get your take

00:04:52.801 --> 00:04:54.767
on a few of the other ones
to see what you're up against.

00:04:54.767 --> 00:04:56.767
-I will say --
I don't want to --

00:04:56.767 --> 00:04:58.834
I don't want to be too honest
here, but you even show me them,

00:04:58.834 --> 00:05:00.534
[bleep] these birds.
-All right, all right.

00:05:00.534 --> 00:05:02.734
I understand, I understand,
I understand, I understand.

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:04.601
-But make your --
make your best case.

00:05:04.601 --> 00:05:06.334
Show me something which
is better than the puteketeke,

00:05:06.334 --> 00:05:07.968
which you're seeing
right in front of you.

00:05:07.968 --> 00:05:09.934
-Yes. Okay.
The puteketeke...

00:05:09.934 --> 00:05:11.100
-Give me another bird.
-Well, how about this?

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:14.767
How about an
eastern rockhopper penguin?

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:16.200
-I mean,
that looks like a hip --

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:17.901
[ Audience groans ]
Exactly!

00:05:17.901 --> 00:05:20.334
You can feel
they're not convinced.

00:05:20.334 --> 00:05:21.801
[ Laughter ]
-No, I think they --

00:05:21.801 --> 00:05:23.767
-No, they're not.
That's a hipster penguin.

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:25.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:25.601 --> 00:05:27.501
What are you -- What are you
bringing to the table here?

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:30.767
What have you got
the puteketeke doesn't?

00:05:30.767 --> 00:05:32.367
-Due to their --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:32.367 --> 00:05:33.868
-Due to their
rocky environment,

00:05:33.868 --> 00:05:35.868
they cannot slide on their
bellies like most penguins,

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:37.868
so they hop -- they hop around.

00:05:37.868 --> 00:05:40.367
-No, that's -- I mean, that is
a charming little detail.

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:41.634
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.634 --> 00:05:44.067
-But I'm willing to bet
that's all you've got.

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:45.667
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.667 --> 00:05:46.834
What else?
What else, Jimmy?

00:05:46.834 --> 00:05:49.234
What other rockhopper facts
have you got?

00:05:49.234 --> 00:05:50.767
-They're excellent --
-Hold on.

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:52.534
Let me -- Let me just
cover up what's written.

00:05:52.534 --> 00:05:53.968
[ Laughter ]

00:05:53.968 --> 00:05:57.000
Tell me something
about this bird, Jimmy.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.167
-I have no --
They weigh 5 to 10 pounds?

00:05:59.167 --> 00:06:00.868
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.868 --> 00:06:02.234
All right, all right,
so this one.

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:03.667
Yeah, this one's out,
but they look kind of cool.

00:06:03.667 --> 00:06:05.434
But I'm going puteketeke.
-Yeah. Thank you.

00:06:05.434 --> 00:06:07.334
-How about this?
How about the tomtit?

00:06:07.334 --> 00:06:09.734
-Oh, please.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:06:09.734 --> 00:06:11.968
Please!
Please, that is a --

00:06:11.968 --> 00:06:14.334
That is a --
That is a basic bee.

00:06:14.334 --> 00:06:17.200
[ Laughter ]

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.467
That's more bee than bird.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:21.868
Pick a lane.
Which do you want to be?

00:06:21.868 --> 00:06:24.234
-I mean, that's
pretty cute, the tomtit.

00:06:24.234 --> 00:06:26.767
-What does it do, Jimmy?
What does it do?

00:06:26.767 --> 00:06:30.667
-Uh, the tomtits are attracted
to humans

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:33.801
because they stir up soil
and disturb insects

00:06:33.801 --> 00:06:36.534
for them to feed on.

00:06:36.534 --> 00:06:38.601
-Okay, so --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:38.601 --> 00:06:40.701
-It started off good.
-Let the people decide.

00:06:40.701 --> 00:06:43.534
Do you want this thing?
Again, bad bee costume.

00:06:43.534 --> 00:06:45.400
Or do you want the puteketeke?

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:47.734 --> 00:06:51.634
That's -- That's what
democracy is all about.

00:06:51.634 --> 00:06:54.667
-The last one I'll just show --
This one is just, uh...

00:06:54.667 --> 00:06:56.934
No, this is interesting.
You could have one here.

00:06:56.934 --> 00:06:59.334
The royal spoonbill.
-Oh [bleep]

00:06:59.334 --> 00:07:02.434
[ Laughter ]

00:07:02.434 --> 00:07:03.634
-That's a --
that's a pretty good bird.

00:07:03.634 --> 00:07:05.234
-That's a decent bird.

00:07:05.234 --> 00:07:08.234
-Are its feathers
always like that,

00:07:08.234 --> 00:07:10.801
or is that, like,
a special occasion?

00:07:10.801 --> 00:07:12.534
Is that after --
after being glammed up?

00:07:12.534 --> 00:07:14.534
[ Laughter ]

00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:16.000
-She just came from the CMAs.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.601
Yeah, exactly. It has --

00:07:18.601 --> 00:07:21.534
-CMA is exactly the right one.
Not the Met Gala.

00:07:21.534 --> 00:07:22.868
-No, no, not --
-It's the CMAs.

00:07:22.868 --> 00:07:25.400
Yes, not the Grammy Awards.
Not the American --

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:27.701
The CMAs one.
I don't know why.

00:07:27.701 --> 00:07:30.200
When they've caught something
to eat, they lift up their bill.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.567
They let the morsel slide
down their throat.

00:07:32.567 --> 00:07:34.834
I don't really like that.
-Yeah.

00:07:34.834 --> 00:07:36.701
The bill has
a vibration detector

00:07:36.701 --> 00:07:39.200
that can detect prey
in murky water,

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.133
allowing the bird
to feed at nighttime.

00:07:41.133 --> 00:07:43.367
-I mean, we're all learning
more about birds

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:45.234
than we thought
we were going to tonight.

00:07:45.234 --> 00:07:46.801
-Yeah, this is interesting.

00:07:46.801 --> 00:07:48.834
-I would not be mad
at losing to that bird.

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:50.234
-Yeah.
-I'll be totally honest.

00:07:50.234 --> 00:07:51.734
I didn't come into this
expecting that,

00:07:51.734 --> 00:07:53.434
but that's a decent bird.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:53.434 --> 00:07:56.801
-But you -- you said you also
like to puteketeke

00:07:56.801 --> 00:07:58.934
because of their mating ritual,
their mating dance.

00:07:58.934 --> 00:08:01.434
-Yeah, they have an incredible
mating dance.

00:08:01.434 --> 00:08:03.033
I believe there's a video of it.

00:08:03.033 --> 00:08:05.767
What I like about it
the most is that they put --

00:08:05.767 --> 00:08:07.000
Well, you'll see.
Do you have a clip

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.801
of this mating dance?
We talked about it.

00:08:08.801 --> 00:08:11.133
-This is John talking
about the mating dance.

00:08:11.133 --> 00:08:14.133
-And you want elegance?
Oh, I'll give you some elegance.

00:08:14.133 --> 00:08:16.767
They have a mating dance
where they both grab a clump

00:08:16.767 --> 00:08:20.801
of wet grass and chest-bump each
other before standing around,

00:08:20.801 --> 00:08:23.934
unsure of what to do next.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.934 --> 00:08:25.934
-All right.
Convinced right there.

00:08:25.934 --> 00:08:27.267
-What's not to like about that?

00:08:27.267 --> 00:08:29.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.367
If you don't identify
with that, you're not

00:08:32.367 --> 00:08:33.467
answering honest questions
about yourself.

00:08:33.467 --> 00:08:35.300
-All right. Good.
Yeah. Yeah, exactly.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:40.000
I wanted to say thanks again
for doing Strike Force Five

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.067
with me over the summer.
-You're welcome, Jimmy.

00:08:42.067 --> 00:08:45.067
I am more than happy
to talk about any other subject

00:08:45.067 --> 00:08:47.234
than birds
while wearing this bird costume.

00:08:47.234 --> 00:08:50.234
[ Laughter ]
-I want you to. I'm so excited.

00:08:50.234 --> 00:08:52.734
I just love it.
You went for it.

00:08:52.734 --> 00:08:54.601
We did a bunch of episodes.

00:08:54.601 --> 00:08:57.601
I got to know you more than --
[ Laughter ]

00:08:57.601 --> 00:09:00.667
more than I ever thought I knew
to get to know you,

00:09:00.667 --> 00:09:02.234
and your beautiful wife,
as well.

00:09:02.234 --> 00:09:04.701
-It was super fun.
-Yeah, and we --

00:09:04.701 --> 00:09:07.200
Episode 5 went a little --
a little haywire.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:08.501
-I mean, it was a masterpiece.

00:09:08.501 --> 00:09:11.400
-It was a masterpiece
of a disaster, wasn't it?

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.067
-Well, did your wife --

00:09:13.067 --> 00:09:14.767
-Well, my wife,
when you started texting

00:09:14.767 --> 00:09:17.834
all of our wives late one night,
and it was late...

00:09:17.834 --> 00:09:22.334
[ Laughter ]

00:09:22.334 --> 00:09:23.601
It was late to have
your wife saying,

00:09:23.601 --> 00:09:25.834
"Jimmy's texting me right now."
But anyway...

00:09:25.834 --> 00:09:27.133
-I shouldn't have
texted that late.

00:09:27.133 --> 00:09:28.968
-She said --
She said to me,

00:09:28.968 --> 00:09:30.934
"I don't know
what these questions mean."

00:09:30.934 --> 00:09:33.400
And I said,
"It can't be that confusing."

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:34.567
And I read them and realized,

00:09:34.567 --> 00:09:36.300
"Oh, [bleep] it was very much
that confusing."

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:37.534
[ Laughter ]

00:09:37.534 --> 00:09:39.000
What I didn't understand
was you --

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.501
you texted different versions
of confusing questions

00:09:41.501 --> 00:09:43.868
to all the different partners.

00:09:43.868 --> 00:09:47.234
So you had layers upon layers
of a debacle

00:09:47.234 --> 00:09:48.601
that was going to unfold.

00:09:48.601 --> 00:09:50.901
And she said, "I don't think
this is going to work."

00:09:50.901 --> 00:09:52.501
And I said,
"I think it is going to work

00:09:52.501 --> 00:09:54.234
but not in the way
that you think it's going to."

00:09:54.234 --> 00:09:56.267
[ Laughter ]
And it did!

00:09:56.267 --> 00:09:58.434
It was the hardest I've laughed
during the strike.

00:09:58.434 --> 00:10:00.567
The strike was a stressful time,
and, boy,

00:10:00.567 --> 00:10:02.467
you gave me
some hard laughs for that.

00:10:02.467 --> 00:10:04.000
-Because we were on these
Zoom screens.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.000
I could see you,
and I could tell

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:06.133
if something made you laugh,

00:10:06.133 --> 00:10:07.601
your head would
fall out of the screen.

00:10:07.601 --> 00:10:09.601
-I was --
I was laughing so hard,

00:10:09.601 --> 00:10:12.234
I wasn't making any sound,
and I was in pain.

00:10:12.234 --> 00:10:14.234
[ Laughter ]
You kind of think,

00:10:14.234 --> 00:10:16.133
"Is this what it feels like
to rupture a spleen?"

00:10:16.133 --> 00:10:18.434
Because I think it's...
[ Laughter ]

00:10:18.434 --> 00:10:20.534
-It was -- it was
one of my favorite experiences.

00:10:20.534 --> 00:10:22.968
What a terrible time and turned
it into something great.

00:10:22.968 --> 00:10:24.868
Raised a lot of money
for our staff and crew.

00:10:24.868 --> 00:10:27.801
-Yeah, it was it was the one
piece of -- scoop of ice cream

00:10:27.801 --> 00:10:29.000
in the middle
of a pile of [bleep]

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:30.300
[ Laughter ]

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:31.868
-I'm so happy you're here.

00:10:31.868 --> 00:10:33.767
And, gosh, thank you,
and best of luck

00:10:33.767 --> 00:10:35.868
to the puteketeke.
-If you're wondering

00:10:35.868 --> 00:10:38.300
if this suit is hot to be in,
the answer is yes.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:40.801
[ Laughter ]
-John Oliver, everybody.

00:10:40.801 --> 00:10:46.133
"Last Week Tonight" airs Sundays
on HBO and streams on Max.

00:10:46.133 --> 00:10:48.901
Matt Rife joins us
after the break.

00:10:48.901 --> 00:10:50.300
Stick around, everybody!

